"
\h0#preface|Preface|

This document describes the IW4x Steam companion (\c{companion}) along
with \c{libcompanion}, the library that it is built from. For the command
line interface of the Linux launcher refer to the \l{iw4x-steam(1)} man
page.

In this document we use the term \i{companion} to refer to the code that
runs in the Steam client and \i{module} to refer to the file that contains
this code, \c{iw4x-steam64.dll} on Windows and \c{libiw4x-steam.so} on
Linux. The \i{game} is always IW4x and the \i{title} is the text that Steam
friends see as the game being played.

\h1#intro|Introduction|

The IW4x Steam companion is a Steam client module that lets IW4x choose
the title, that is, the line under the player's name in the friends list of
each of their Steam friends. The game sets the title, for example,
\c{IW4x: Modern Warfare 2}, and can change it at any time while it runs.

Without Companion the game has no say in the title. Steam shows friends
the Steam app that IW4x runs as (Call of Duty: Modern Warfare 2 or
Spacewar) or, if the game is started from a non-Steam shortcut, the name of
the shortcut.

So how can a module in the Steam client change what friends see? The client
reports the running games to the CM server (the Steam server that the
client stays connected to) with the \c{GamesPlayed} message, one entry per
game, and the CM server passes each entry on to friends. Companion
intercepts this message on its way out and, in each entry that refers to a
running IW4x process, sets the title to the one that the game provides. The
CM server only shows a Steam app that the account owns, so Companion
also sets the app to Call of Duty: Modern Warfare 2 (app 10190) or, if the
account does not own it, to Spacewar (app 480), which every account owns.
Every other message goes out unchanged.

For this to work, the game and Companion need to know about each other.
The game registers with Companion by publishing a small record with the
app id and the title. Companion, in turn, publishes its status, which
tells the game whether Companion is running and whether it managed to
hook the Steam client. We call this exchange the \i{game protocol} (see
\l{#protocol Game Protocol} for details).

There is a companion for each Steam client that IW4x runs with:

\dl|

\li|\c{\b{iw4x-steam64.dll}}

The Windows module for the 64-bit Windows Steam client. The game ships this
module next to \c{iw4x.dll} and loads it into the running Steam client, so
there is nothing to install.|

\li|\c{\b{libiw4x-steam.so}}

The Linux module for the 32-bit native Linux Steam client, which runs the
game under Proton. The Steam client can only load this module when it
starts. This is why the module is installed together with the
\c{iw4x-steam} launcher, which starts the Steam client with the module
preloaded.||

Both modules are built from \c{libcompanion}, which contains the
platform-independent parts of Companion. These are the parsing of the
Steam client library and the location of the function to hook along with
the decoding of the records and the rewriting of the reports. The library
also contains the vendored MinHook library. The Windows module uses its
hooking engine and both modules use its instruction decoder. The hooking
engine is only built for Windows and the rest of the library has no
dependency on either operating system. As a result, the library is built and
tested on any host.

We begin with a walkthrough that installs the Linux companion and then look
at how Companion works in more detail. The final chapters describe the
game protocol and the use of the library.

\h#intro-linux|Installing on Linux|

On Windows there is nothing to install since the game loads Companion
into the Steam client every time it starts. So let's set up the Linux
companion.

The Linux module requires glibc 2.36 or later (Debian 12, for example).
Building it requires GCC 16 or later together with the 32-bit glibc and
libstdc++ development files. On Fedora, for example, these are the
\c{glibc-devel.i686} and \c{libstdc++-devel.i686} packages.

The Steam client is a 32-bit program and the programs that start it are
64-bit. So we install Companion from two build configurations into the
same location. The i386 configuration provides the module and the launcher
and the x86_64 configuration provides the empty 64-bit stand-ins of the
module. We will see what the stand-ins are for in \l{#linux-preload
Preloading}.

Let's create the two configurations. The installation filter limits the
installation to what the Steam client needs and leaves out the library's
headers and \c{pkg-config} files:

\
$ f='lib/iw4x-steam/@true lib/@false include/@false'

$ bpkg create -d companion-i686 cc   \\
  config.cxx='g++ -m32'            \\
  config.cxx.target=i686-linux-gnu \\
  config.c='gcc -m32'              \\
  config.c.target=i686-linux-gnu   \\
  config.cc.coptions=-O2           \\
  config.install.root=/usr/local   \\
  config.install.sudo=sudo         \\
  config.install.filter=\"$f\"

$ bpkg create -d companion-x86_64 cc \\
  config.cxx=g++                   \\
  config.cc.coptions=-O2           \\
  config.install.root=/usr/local   \\
  config.install.sudo=sudo         \\
  config.install.filter=\"$f\"
\

\N|GCC only reports the i386 target that \c{-m32} selects if it is built
with multiarch support (Debian, Ubuntu). This is why we specify the target
explicitly.|

Now we can build and install Companion in each configuration:

\
$ bpkg build -d companion-i686 \\
  libcompanion@https://github.com/iw4x/companion.git#main
$ bpkg install -d companion-i686 libcompanion

$ bpkg build -d companion-x86_64 \\
  libcompanion@https://github.com/iw4x/companion.git#main
$ bpkg install -d companion-x86_64 libcompanion
\

Let's start Steam with Companion. The module can only be loaded when
the Steam client starts, so if Steam is already running, we exit it first:

\
$ iw4x-steam
\

\N|The launcher passes its arguments to \c{steam} and refuses to start if
Steam is still running. See \l{iw4x-steam(1)} for details.|

Companion writes its diagnostics to the Steam client's \c{stderr},
which the client copies to its console log. Let's see what we've got:

\
$ grep -o '\\[iw4x-steam\\].*' ~/.steam/steam/logs/console-linux.txt
[iw4x-steam] send function at steamclient.so+0x2dca890 (1 references)
[iw4x-steam] hooked the send function (9 bytes relocated)
[iw4x-steam] active (build 2)
\

The first line tells us that Companion found the function through
which the Steam client sends its messages, at this offset in
\c{steamclient.so}. The second line says that Companion hooked this
function and the last one that it is active. From this point on the
companion is idle until a game registers.

Now let's start the game and look at the log again:

\
[iw4x-steam] record changed
[iw4x-steam] resent GamesPlayed on connection 0xdced7cd0 (294 -> 308
  bytes, after a frame on 0xdce81d40)
\

What happened here? Steam reported the game right after starting it, before
the game had a chance to register. Once the game published its record, the
companion rewrote the last report and sent it again. From now on our
friends see the title that the game provides.

\h1#operation|Companion Operation|

In this chapter we look at what Companion does inside the Steam
client, which is the same on both platforms. How Companion gets into
the Steam client and how it finds the record of a game are platform-specific
and are the subject of the next two chapters.

\h#operation-startup|Startup|

Once in the Steam client, Companion performs the following steps on a
thread of its own:

\ol|

\li|Publish the status with the \c{starting} state. Publishing the status
also makes sure that there is a single companion: a second copy of the
module finds the status already published and does nothing.|

\li|Find the send function in the Steam client library,
\c{steamclient64.dll} on Windows and \c{steamclient.so} on Linux.|

\li|Hook the send function.|

\li|Start watching the records for changes.|

\li|Publish the \c{active} state.||

If Companion does not recognize the Steam client library, then it
publishes the \c{unsupported} state. If it fails to hook the send function,
then it publishes the \c{failed} state. In either case it does nothing
further and the Steam client continues as if the module was never loaded.

\h#operation-locate|Locating the Send Function|

The Steam client sends every message to the CM server through the
\c{CWebSocketConnection::BBuildAndAsyncSendFrame()} function. The Steam
client library does not export this function and its machine code changes
with almost every Steam build. So how does Companion find it? By its
structure.

The function opens a profiler scope named after itself. As a result, the
library contains the function's name as a string, which we call the
\i{anchor}, and the function loads the anchor's address. Companion
parses the library image and finds every instruction that loads this
address. It then maps each such instruction to the function that contains
it using the unwind information.

Which of these functions is the send function depends on the architecture.
On x64 the functions that inline the send function also load the anchor and
the send function is the one that loads it within 256 bytes of its entry,
right after its prologue. On i386 the address is computed relative to the
global offset table and the send function is the one that loads the
table's address itself.

The search must end with exactly one function. Any other result means a
Steam build that Companion does not recognize and that it reports as
\c{unsupported}. The diagnostics include the number of references and
candidates that were found, for example:

\
[iw4x-steam] unable to locate the send function: ambiguous (4
  references, 2 candidates)
\

\h#operation-hook|Send Hook|

Companion hooks the send function with a \i{detour}: it overwrites the
start of the function with a jump to its own code, which calls the original
function through a \i{trampoline}. On Windows the detour is installed with
the MinHook hooking engine. On Linux the Steam client is 32-bit and the
companion builds its own i386 detour. It decodes the overwritten
instructions with the MinHook instruction decoder and refuses to install
the detour if they contain anything that it cannot relocate.

The hook runs on the Steam client's network thread for every message that
the client sends, so the common case must be cheap. The hook recognizes a
\c{GamesPlayed} message by its header with a few comparisons and passes
every other message to the original function as is.

\h#operation-rewrite|Report Rewriting|

A \c{GamesPlayed} report contains a \c{GamePlayed} entry for each running
game. The entry includes the game id and the id of the process that Steam
tracks for this game. For each entry Companion looks for the record
that the game in this process has published (see \l{#protocol Game
Protocol}). If there is one, then Companion sets the game id to the app
id from the record and the game's extra information to the title from the
record. It is this extra information that the CM server shows to friends.

The rest of the report is copied byte for byte. A report without entries of
registered processes is sent unchanged. So is a report larger than 8192
bytes, which is far beyond any realistic report.

\h#operation-resend|Report Resending|

Steam reports a game right after starting it, normally before the game has
published its record. After that Steam only sends a new report when the set
of running games changes. The game can also update its record while it
runs, for example, to switch from Modern Warfare 2 to Spacewar. Without
further measures friends would therefore not see the title until something
else changes.

To handle this, Companion keeps the last report together with the
message that was actually sent for it and watches the records for changes.
On a change it rewrites the report again and, if the result differs from
what the CM server last received, sends it on the same connection.

When exactly is the report resent? Companion has no thread of its own
in the Steam client's networking code. So the resend happens when the Steam
client sends its next message on the thread that sent the last report.
Steam uses a single thread per connection, which means the resend never
interleaves with the client's own messages on this connection. If the
connection has been idle for 60 seconds, then Companion assumes it is
closed and drops the report.

\h1#linux|Linux Companion|

The native Linux Steam client runs Windows games with Proton, in a
container provided by the Steam Runtime. In this chapter we look at how the
companion gets into the Steam client and how it finds the record of a game
that runs in such a container.

\h#linux-preload|Preloading|

The Steam client does not load plugins, so the module is loaded with
\c{LD_PRELOAD}. This is why the module must be in place when the Steam
client starts and why the Steam client is started with the \c{iw4x-steam}
launcher.

A chain of 64-bit programs starts the Steam client and they all inherit
\c{LD_PRELOAD}. A 64-bit program cannot load the 32-bit module and its
dynamic linker warns about every program that it fails to preload. To avoid
these warnings, the launcher refers to the module through the dynamic
linker's \c{$PLATFORM} token:

\
LD_PRELOAD=/usr/local/lib/iw4x-steam/$PLATFORM/libiw4x-steam.so
\

The dynamic linker expands \c{$PLATFORM} in each process separately. In
the 32-bit Steam client it expands to \c{i686}, the directory that contains
the module. In a 64-bit program it expands to \c{x86_64} or, on some CPUs,
to a more specific name (\c{haswell}, \c{xeon_phi}). Each of these
directories contains an empty 64-bit stand-in.

In every process that it is loaded into, the module first removes itself
from \c{LD_PRELOAD}. As a result, the processes that this one starts in turn
(Proton and the games, for example) do not load it. In any process other
than the Steam client, which the module recognizes by its executable name
(\c{steam}), this is all that the module does.

\h#linux-startup|Startup|

Companion publishes its status in the
\c{/dev/shm/iw4x-steam-companion.\i{uid}} file, where \i{uid} is the user
id, and keeps this file locked while it runs. Another companion of the same
user, for example, one in a second Steam client, fails to lock the file and
does nothing:

\
[iw4x-steam] another companion is resident, staying inert
\

The Steam client loads \c{steamclient.so} once its bootstrapper finishes
updating it, which can take a while on a slow connection. So Companion
waits for up to 30 minutes for the library to load before it gives up and
publishes the \c{unsupported} state.

When the Steam client exits, Companion removes the status file. If the
client crashes, then the file is left behind and the next companion takes
it over. In the meantime the game recognizes the status as stale by the
Steam client's process id that it contains.

\h#linux-lookup|Game Lookup|

The native Steam client cannot see the Windows objects of a game that runs
under Wine. So the game publishes its record as a file in \c{/dev/shm},
which the Proton container shares with the host. This leaves the question
of which file belongs to which report.

Steam reports a game with the id of the process that it tracks for the
game, which is one of Steam's own helpers or one of Proton's processes. The
game itself cannot find out its Linux process id since Wine performs its
file operations in the \c{wineserver} process of its prefix. So the game
names its record after the process id of this \c{wineserver}, which Proton
starts in the process tree that Steam tracks.

To find the record for a report, Companion searches the reported
process and its descendants, breadth-first. For each process it tries the
record under each of the process's ids, one per process id namespace that
the process is in, since the game sees the ids of the container. The record
also contains the start time of the \c{wineserver} process, which tells a
current record apart from a stale one left by an earlier process with the
same id.

A game under Proton consists of a few dozen processes. The search visits at
most 256 of them and issues diagnostics if it runs out of processes to
visit:

\
[iw4x-steam] no record for process 4242 (overflow)
\

It also issues diagnostics if the reported process is the Steam client or
one of its ancestors (\c{related}). The tree of such a process contains
every game and is therefore not searched.

\h#linux-glibc|glibc Compatibility|

The module runs in the Steam client and therefore with the glibc of the
system. It links the C++ runtime statically and binds its glibc references
to versions no newer than 2.36. As a result, one build runs on any
distribution with glibc 2.36 or later, which the module's tests verify.

\h1#windows|Windows Companion|

On Windows the Steam client is a 64-bit program and the game is a 32-bit
one. A 32-bit process cannot load a module into a 64-bit one, so the game
enlists the help of the 64-bit \c{rundll32.exe}.

\h#windows-attach|Attaching|

The game attaches Companion to the Steam client by running the
module's \c{IW4xAttach} entry point in \c{rundll32.exe} and passing the
Steam client's process id:

\
rundll32.exe \"...\\iw4x-steam64.dll\",IW4xAttach 5120
\

The entry point verifies that the process is a 64-bit Steam client and
loads the module into it by calling \c{LoadLibraryW()} on a remote thread.
It then waits for Companion to settle and returns the outcome as the
\c{rundll32.exe} exit code:

\dl|

\li|\c{\b{0} active}

Companion is active.|

\li|\c{\b{1} unsupported}

Companion does not recognize the Steam client library.|

\li|\c{\b{2} failed}

Companion failed to hook the send function.|

\li|\c{\b{3} invalid_arguments}

The command line does not contain a valid process id.|

\li|\c{\b{4} not_steam}

The process is not a 64-bit Steam client.|

\li|\c{\b{5} access_denied}

The process cannot be opened, for example, because it runs with higher
privileges.|

\li|\c{\b{6} injection_failed}

The module could not be loaded into the process.|

\li|\c{\b{7} timeout}

The module was not loaded within 10 seconds or Companion did not settle
within 5 seconds.||

If Companion is already running in the Steam client, for example, from
an earlier game session, then the entry point reports its state without
loading anything.

\h#windows-startup|Startup|

Companion publishes its status in a named file mapping whose name
includes the Steam client's process id. Creating this mapping is also what
makes sure that there is a single companion per Steam client. Companion
then finds the send function in \c{steamclient64.dll} and proceeds as
described in \l{#operation-startup Startup}.

\N|The Steam client loads \c{steamclient64.dll} before any game can start.
If the library is not loaded, then Companion publishes the
\c{unsupported} state.|

The module stays loaded until the Steam client exits since, once the hook
is installed, the client executes the module's code.

The game publishes its record in a named file mapping of its own and
signals a named event after every change to the record. This event is how
Companion learns about the change.

Companion writes its diagnostics to the debugger output, where each
line starts with \c{[iw4x-steam64]}. To view them, use a tool such as
DebugView.

\h1#protocol|Game Protocol|

The game protocol defines how the game and Companion communicate. It
is declared in \c{<libcompanion/protocol.hxx>}, which only depends on the
standard library so that the game can include it directly.

\h#protocol-compat|Compatibility|

The game and each of the two companions are built with their own toolchains
for their own architectures and are upgraded on their own schedules. The
companion also stays loaded until the Steam client exits. As a result, any
version of the game can meet any version of Companion.

This is why every structure of the protocol has a fixed layout of 32-bit
fields and starts with a magic followed by its version and size. A reader
only accepts the exact layout that it knows and every change to a layout
increments its version. In addition, Companion publishes its build
number, which is incremented with every change in Companion's behavior
that the game can observe. The current build is 2.

\h#protocol-record|Registration Record|

The game registers by publishing a record with the app id to report and the
title to show:

\
struct record
{
  uint32_t magic;          // 0x50345749 (\"IW4P\")
  uint32_t version;        // 1
  uint32_t size;           // 84
  uint32_t process_id;     // Game process id.
  uint32_t app_id;
  char     extra_info[64]; // Title, UTF-8, NUL-terminated.
};
\

The app id must be non-zero and fit into 24 bits. The title must be
non-empty and valid UTF-8 without control characters. It must also fit
into the 64 bytes together with the terminating \c{NUL}. The game can
update both while it runs. For example, it starts with Modern Warfare 2
(app 10190) and switches to Spacewar (app 480) if Steam reports that the
account does not own it.

On Windows the record is published in the
\c{Local\\IW4x.SteamPresence.}\i{pid} named file mapping, where \i{pid} is
the game's process id, which the record repeats. The mapping exists while
the game holds a handle to it, so the record goes away with the game. The
game writes the magic last with release ordering and Companion reads it
first with acquire ordering. As a result, a record with the right magic is
always complete. After every change to the record the game sets the
\c{Local\\IW4x.SteamPresence.Changed.}\i{steam-pid} auto-reset event, where
\i{steam-pid} is the Steam client's process id.

On Linux the record is published in the \c{/dev/shm/iw4x-presence.}\i{pid}
file, where \i{pid} is the process id of the game's \c{wineserver} (see
\l{#linux-lookup Game Lookup}). The game opens this file through Wine's
\c{\\\\?\\unix\\} path prefix and writes it in one go. The Linux record has
two more fields that hold the \c{wineserver} start time, which is field 22
of \c{/proc/}\i{pid}\c{/stat}:

\
struct unix_record
{
  uint32_t magic;           // 0x55345749 (\"IW4U\")
  uint32_t version;         // 1
  uint32_t size;            // 92
  uint32_t process_id;      // wineserver process id.
  uint32_t start_time_low;  // wineserver start time.
  uint32_t start_time_high;
  uint32_t app_id;
  char     extra_info[64];
};
\

\h#protocol-status|Companion Status|

Companion publishes its status, which the game reads to find out
whether Companion is running and in which state. The state has one of
the following values:

\dl|

\li|\c{\b{0} starting}

Companion is initializing.|

\li|\c{\b{1} active}

The send hook is installed and the records are applied.|

\li|\c{\b{2} unsupported}

Companion does not recognize the Steam client library.|

\li|\c{\b{3} failed}

Companion failed to hook the send function.||

On Windows the status is published in the
\c{Local\\IW4x.SteamCompanion.}\i{steam-pid} named file mapping, where
\i{steam-pid} is the Steam client's process id. Companion stores the
state last with release ordering and the game loads it first with acquire
ordering:

\
struct status
{
  uint32_t magic;   // 0x53345749 (\"IW4S\")
  uint32_t version; // 1
  uint32_t size;    // 24
  uint32_t state;
  uint32_t build;   // Companion build.
  uint32_t hook;    // Send function offset or 0.
};
\

On Linux the status is published in the
\c{/dev/shm/iw4x-steam-companion.}\i{uid} file, where \i{uid} is the real
user id, which the game reads from \c{/proc/self/status}. The Linux status
also contains the Steam client's process id, which lets the game recognize
a status left behind by a crashed client:

\
struct unix_status
{
  uint32_t magic;     // 0x54345749 (\"IW4T\")
  uint32_t version;   // 1
  uint32_t size;      // 28
  uint32_t state;
  uint32_t build;
  uint32_t hook;
  uint32_t steam_pid; // Steam client process id.
};
\

\h1#usage|Usage|

The \c{libcompanion} package is available from its \c{git} repository. To
install the Linux companion, follow the steps in \l{#intro-linux
Installing on Linux}.

To start using \c{libcompanion} in your project, add the following
\c{depends} value to your \c{manifest}, adjusting the version constraint
as appropriate:

\
depends: libcompanion ^0.1.0
\

Then import the library in your \c{buildfile}:

\
import libs = libcompanion%lib{companion}
\

Note that the library interface is internal to Companion and is not
stable. The game protocol (\c{<libcompanion/protocol.hxx>}) is the
exception and follows the rules described in \l{#protocol-compat
Compatibility}.

The package provides the following importable targets:

\
lib{companion}
libs{iw4x-steam64}
libs{iw4x-steam}
exe{iw4x-steam}
\

The \c{lib{companion\}} library contains the platform-independent parts of
Companion along with MinHook, whose headers are installed into the
\c{libcompanion/minhook/} subdirectory. The instruction decoder headers,
\c{hde32.h} and \c{hde64.h}, are installed on every target and the hooking
engine header, \c{minhook.h}, only on Windows. The \c{libs{iw4x-steam64\}}
target is the Windows module and is only built for x86_64 Windows. The
\c{libs{iw4x-steam\}} target is the Linux module and \c{exe{iw4x-steam\}}
is its launcher. Both are only built for i386 Linux and the launcher only
when installing.
"
